  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 133 PM CDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nThrough Monday: Continued dry with cooler than normal morning lows \nand slightly warmer than normal afternoon highs Monday. Surface \nridging will continue across the region from the northeast resulting \nin an east wind and keeping the Gulf closed through Monday. Ridging \naloft will remain over the region as well. Together these features \nwill result in decent radiational cooling tonight and near full \ninsolation Monday. The result will be critically low afternoon \nminimum relative humidity maintaining fire danger concerns. /22/\n\nMonday Night through Saturday...High pressure at the surface \ncentered to our north and increasing ridging aloft from the west \nthrough the week, will maintain quiet weather across the forecast \narea.  The overall dry air mass will remain across the region \nthrough the week as dew points generally mix into the 30s & 40s each \nafternoon.  However, temperatures will steadily warm each day. This \nwarming will be felt more with daytime highs, than with overnight \nlows.  Lows through the period will generally range from the mid 40s \nto mid 50s.  Meanwhile highs on Tuesday will range from the upper \n70s to middle 80s.  Then by Saturday, they'll have increased into \nthe lower 80s to around 90.\n\nThese increasing high temperatures in this dry air mass will result \nin afternoon minimum relative humidity values falling to critically \nlow values, i.e. generally into the 20-30% range.  This, coupled \nwith the extremely dry soil conditions across the area will continue \nto promote at least a \"Limited Threat\" for dangerous fire conditions \nacross the forecast area through the period.  Please continue to \nmonitor the latest forecast and outlook through the week, as some \ncategorical adjustment could be made to portions of, if not all of, \nthe forecast area. /19/",
